{{en-adj|-|nolinkhead=1}}

# {{lb|en|of a capture in chess}} Performed by a [[pawn#Noun|pawn]] on an enemy pawn that has just passed over its attack zone.

===Adverb===
{{en-adv|-|nolinkhead=1}}

# [[in passing|In passing]], [[by the way]], [[incidentally]]. {{defdate|from early 17th c.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=[Miscellaneous.] Art. 65. ''The Turtle Dove.'' A Tale, from the French of M. de Florian. 8vo. pp. 25. 1s. Printed at Caen in Normandy; and Sold in London by Payne [book review]|journal=[[w:Monthly Review (London)|The Monthly Review; or, Literary Journal, Enlarged]]|location=London|publisher=Printed for [[w:Ralph Griffiths|R[alph] Griffiths]]; and sold by T[homas] Becket, in [[w:Pall Mall, London|Pall Mall]]|month=January|year=1791|volume=IV|page=113|pageurl=https://archive.org/stream/monthlyreview41unkngoog#page/n127/mode/1up|oclc=901376714|passage=Turtle doves have long been celebrated for their fidelity; and ''this'' turtle dove, though he flirts, '''''en paſſant''''', with a ''ſky-lark'', a ''jay'', and a ''quail'', does not materially diſcredit the famed conſtancy of his ſpecies, theſe birds being all ''coquettes'': but when he meets (as he fortunately does) with an amiable dove-mate, he is as faithful as any turtle, of any grove.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|title=Sheriff of Dublin—Inquiry into His Conduct|editor=[[w:Thomas Curson Hansard|T[homas] C[urson] Hansard]]|journal=[[w:Hansard#In the United Kingdom|The Parliamentary Debates: Forming a Continuation of the Work Entitled “The Parliamentary History of England from the Earliest Period to the Year 1803.”]]|series=New Series; Commencing with the Accession of [[w:George IV of the United Kingdom|George IV]]|location=London|publisher=Printed by T. C. Hansard at the [[w:Paternoster Row|Pater-noster-Row]] Press; for Baldwin, Cradock, and Joy [''et al.'']|date=6 May 1823|year_published=1824|volume=IX (Comprising the Period from the First Day of May, to the Nineteenth Day of July, 1823)|section=columns 71–72|sectionurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=W549AAAAcAAJ&pg=PA71|oclc=729577511|passage=Mr. ''William Poole'' called in, and examined {{...}} I spoke to sheriff Thorpe, '''en passant''', one day in Sackville-street, saying, "I should wish to be on the next commission jury;" and he said, it should be so.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Victor-Joseph Étienne de Jouy|[Victor-Joseph Étienne] de Jouy]]|chapter=The Conclave|title=The Hermit in Italy, or Observations on the Manners and Customs of Italy; being a Continuation of the Sketches of French Manners, [...] In Three Volumes|location=London|publisher=Printed for [[w:George Byrom Whittaker|Geo[rge] B[yrom] Whittaker]], [[w:Ave Maria Lane|Ave-Maria Lane]]|year=1825|volume=I|pages=170–171|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=090GAAAAQAAJ&pg=RA2-PA171|oclc=266071230|passage=[H]e [the newly elected Pope] is placed on the altar, where all the cardinals, according to their rank, pay their reverence to him, and kiss his feet, hands, and mouth. I should observe, '''''en passant''''', that cardinals only have the right to kiss the Pope's hand.}}
# {{lb|en|of capturing an enemy pawn in chess}} By moving one's pawn to the square that the enemy pawn has just passed over.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Mark A. Borders|chapter=The Rules|title=The Self-Improvement of Chess: Why the Game's Basics Apply to Daily Living|location=[Raleigh, N.C.]|publisher=[[w:Lulu (company)|Lulu]]|year=2007|page=12|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=5t9KYWJJyw8C&pg=PA12|isbn=978-1-4303-2764-6|passage=[I]f the opponent chooses to make this capture, he must do it ''immediately'' on his next move&nbsp;– if he makes any other move on his next move, he forever loses the chance to capture ''that'' pawn '''''en passant''''', though he still retains the right to capture ''another'' pawn '''''en passant''''' should a similar situation arise elsewhere.}}

