===Noun===
{{en-noun|nolinkhead=1}}

# {{lb|en|chess}} A [[move#Noun|move]] in which a [[pawn#Noun|pawn]] [[capture#Verb|captures]] an [[opposing#Adjective|opposing]] pawn on the same [[rank#Noun|rank]] immediately after the latter has moved [[forward]] two [[square#Noun|squares]] on its first move in the [[game#Noun|game]], as if it had moved forward only one square.
#* {{quote-journal|1=en|author=Glenn Kaplan|title=Chess: not just for losers, but also Trekkies|url=http://www.oberlin.edu/stupub/ocreview/archives/1998.12.11/sports/trekkies.html|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20170122202504/http://www.oberlin.edu/stupub/ocreview/archives/1998.12.11/sports/trekkies.html|archivedate=22 January 2017|magazine=w:The Oberlin Review|location=Oberlin, Oh.|publisher=The Union Library Association|date=11 December 1998|volume=127|issue=12|issn=0029-7526|oclc=4123706|passage=Spectators are privy to awe inspiring castles, baffling forks, and breathtaking '''en passants'''.|accessdate=9 February 2017}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Mark A. Borders|chapter=The Rules|title=The Self-Improvement of Chess: Why the Game's Basics Apply to Daily Living|location=[Raleigh, N.C.]|publisher=[[w:Lulu (company)|Lulu]]|year=2007|page=12|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=5t9KYWJJyw8C&pg=PA12|isbn=978-1-4303-2764-6|passage='''En passant''' is the rarest of moves. It is a French phrase that means "in passing." This occurs when one player moves a pawn two spaces forward, on its first move, to try to avoid a capture by an opponent pawn. When this happens, the opponent may move his pawn diagonally to the square that the first player passed over (i.e., ''as if'' he only moved one space forward). The pawn from the first player is then considered captured and removed from the board.}}
#* {{quote-web|1=en|author=Ben Watanabe|title=New Mexico Point Guard Kendall Williams Puts Chess Match on Hold During Tournament|url=http://nesn.com/2012/03/new-mexico-point-guard-kendall-williams-puts-chess-match-on-hold-during-tournament/|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20160305012000/http://nesn.com/2012/03/new-mexico-point-guard-kendall-williams-puts-chess-match-on-hold-during-tournament/|archivedate=5 March 2016|work=w:New England Sports Network|date=17 March 2012|passage=Before he returns to performing some castles and '''en passants''', [[w:Kendall Williams|[Kendall] Williams]] has to determine his endgame in the tourney.|accessdate=9 February 2017}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Greg Amos|title=Trumpeter Classic chess tournament begins today|newspaper=w:Grande Prairie Daily Herald-Tribune|url=http://www.dailyheraldtribune.com/2012/05/31/trumpeter-classic-chess-tournament-begins-today<!--Cannot be archived at Archive.org due to robots.txt-->|location=Grande Prairie, Alta.|publisher=w:Sun Media|date=31 May 2012|issn=0839-4873|oclc=462093897|passage=Grande Prairie's Sandman Hotel will be the scene of some castling, '''en passants''' and checkmate action this weekend when the 29th Trumpeter Classic Chess Tournament gets underway.}}

